WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:09.170
26 February 1979.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:12.500
Queen Elizabeth the Second officially opens
Dubai's first skyscraper.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.510
It is the Sheikh Rashid Tower, later known
as the Dubai World Trade Center.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:20.310
VisualPolitik community, at the time, it was
the tallest tower in the whole of the Middle

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:21.310
East.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:22.310
The amazing thing?

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:25.440
That it was erected in the middle of the desert,
in a place where, as you can see, there wasn't

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.440
much of anything.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.820
So the operation didn't seem to make much
sense.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:31.100
Nevertheless, it was quite a statement of
intent.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:36.770
With 39 floors and at 184 meters, that’s
over 600 feet high, this skyscraper made clear

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:41.230
the intentions of the new Dubai that was beginning
to take shape: to become a key center for

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:42.910
business in the Middle East.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:44.190
That was the goal.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:50.890
Today, five decades later, that dream has
come true.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:54.730
Dubai has become one of the greatest examples
of transformation, development and economic

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:56.040
success in history.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.109
And no, these are not just words.

00:00:58.109 --> 00:01:02.030
These days, this emirate-city is home to the
world's tallest building, the largest shopping

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:06.800
mall on the planet, some of the most luxurious
hotels, one of the largest international airports,

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.540
a world-scale mega-port and nearly 400 skyscrapers.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:14.651
That means that since the Dubai World Trade
Center opened, Dubai has built nearly 10 new

00:01:14.651 --> 00:01:16.540
skyscrapers every year.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:17.540
And that's not all.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:21.720
Dubai also tops the list for having the most
supertall skyscrapers in the world, that is,

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.450
towers over 300 meters, or 900 feet high.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.680
No other city comes close.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.930
And above all, it has become a real magnet
for attracting tourists, companies and businesses

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:33.189
from all over the world.

00:01:33.189 --> 00:01:35.040
And, if Dubai's history is dazzling...

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.610
Singapore's is not far behind.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:44.549
This city-state is one of the richest, most
prosperous and successful metropolises in

00:01:44.549 --> 00:01:45.549
the world.

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:49.009
In the last six decades and despite having
fewer than six million inhabitants, it has

00:01:49.009 --> 00:01:53.409
gone from being a poor place to becoming an
important financial, industrial and commercial

00:01:53.409 --> 00:01:54.409
center.

00:01:54.409 --> 00:01:57.799
As a result, Singapore has become one of the
wealthiest and most developed countries in

00:01:57.799 --> 00:01:58.799
the world.

00:01:58.799 --> 00:02:02.890
In terms of GDP per capita, and excluding
microstates, the IMF ranks it as the fourth

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.540
richest country on the planet.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:09.720
We are talking about an estimated GDP per
capita for 2023 of more than $90,000 dollars.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:14.129
And, take note, because this city, despite
its tiny size, has one of the best-equipped

00:02:14.129 --> 00:02:15.819
armed forces on the planet.

00:02:15.819 --> 00:02:20.610
For example, Singapore has more fighter planes
than the air forces of countries such as Canada,

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:22.480
Sweden or the Netherlands.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:23.480
Crazy!

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:26.140
Whichever way you look at it, the figures
of these two metropolises are incredible.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:31.690
In fact, the Globalization and World Cities
Research Network ranks them as no less than

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:36.170
two of the nine most influential and economically
significant cities in the world.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:40.620
They are only one step behind New York and
London, the two great world capitals.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.050
Look at this.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:48.530
And precisely for that reason, here on VisualPolitik
we have already told you in several videos

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:54.390
how Dubai and Singapore managed to take off
and become real economic, commercial and financial

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:55.390
rockets.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:58.720
Basically, we are talking about two cities
that became kind of company-cities.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.300
In fact, it is no coincidence that the airports
of these two cities rank among the busiest

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.300
worldwide.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:10.019
For instance, in 2019, the last year before
the pandemic, Dubai airport moved 97.3 million

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:14.090
passengers and Singapore airport moved 68.3
million.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:16.250
You will see that this is no accident.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:20.160
As it happens, we failed to notice one of
the crucial elements of this whole process:

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.409
the conversion of the governments of these
two cities into true entrepreneurs.

00:03:24.409 --> 00:03:25.510
And the question is,

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:29.489
To what extent was government entrepreneurship
important in the growth and development of

00:03:29.489 --> 00:03:31.310
these two major economic hubs?

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:34.299
What exactly are we talking about when we
say that the governments of these cities were

00:03:34.299 --> 00:03:35.830
truly entrepreneurial?

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:40.189
Can the model that has been so successful
in Dubai and Singapore be exported to other

00:03:40.189 --> 00:03:41.470
less wealthy places?

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:45.370
VisualPolitik community, in this second video
of the series we are preparing in collaboration

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:48.519
with Harvard International Review we answer
all these questions.

00:03:48.519 --> 00:03:51.290
We’ll leave you more information in the
links in the description.

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:55.550
So without further ado, let's get started.

00:03:55.550 --> 00:04:05.709
(PILLARS IN THE SAND: FROM THE DESERT TO THE
WORLD)

00:04:05.709 --> 00:04:10.640
In 1997, Mohamed Alabbar, former Director
General of the Department of Economic Development

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:16.250
of Dubai, was given an assignment: to found
Emaar properties, today one of the most recognized

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:18.720
and most visible brands in all of Dubai.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.100
Its name is everywhere.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.960
And that’s hardly surprising given that,
it is the developer that has carried out the

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:30.110
largest projects in the city: the development
of Downtown, the Burj Khalifa, the Dubai Mall,

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:32.380
Dubai Marina and many of the luxury hotels.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:35.960
We are talking about a company that has developed
most of the projects that have shaped the

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:37.000
current Dubai.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.520
Practically everything that is characteristic
of this city has been promoted by Emaar.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.090
But who is behind this developer?

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:47.210
Well, as many of you can imagine, the commission
to start up this company came from the government

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:48.840
of the Dubai emirate itself.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:53.410
The same government that in 2000 listed the
company on the local stock exchange and that

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:59.259
currently still controls almost 30% of a company
that over the years has become a multinational.

00:04:59.259 --> 00:05:03.990
Today Emaar has activities in a lot of different
countries, thanks to which it had a net profit

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:06.350
of almost $7 billion in 2022.

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:12.090
But, of course, the company's crown jewel
remains Dubai, a city where projects keep

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:13.090
coming.

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:16.370
One of its latest major projects is precisely
the development of Dubai Creek Harbour, a

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:20.780
huge new district that in the coming years
is sure to become one of the city's major

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:21.780
attractions.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:27.160
Incidentally, most of the government's participation
is channeled through ICB, the Investment Corporation

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:32.400
of Dubai, the investment muscle of the Dubai
government, which in addition to Emaar, controls

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.819
or has investments in many, many other companies.

00:05:35.819 --> 00:05:40.550
In fact, the Investment Corporation of Dubai
has had a lot to do with the success of this

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:41.710
city.

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:48.230
So you see, we are talking about an investment
arm of the government that controls assets

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:50.840
worth more than $300 billion dollars.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:55.590
It owns or has stakes in many of the main
local companies such as the Emirates NBD bank,

00:05:55.590 --> 00:06:01.669
the Dubai Islamic Bank, the airlines Emirates
and flyDubai along with the developer Emaar,

00:06:01.669 --> 00:06:05.970
which we have already mentioned, as well as
more than 60 companies in which it has total

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:07.389
or partial stakes.

00:06:07.389 --> 00:06:09.730
So where does the money they invest come from?

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:14.370
Well, basically from issuing debt, reinvesting
profits and public account surpluses.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:18.280
But the most interesting thing is that over
the years, the government of Dubai has used

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:22.410
this partnership to promote business in the
city or to cover those areas that are important

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:23.800
for general development.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.470
Yet it did so following a strict market policy.

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:32.020
The companies participating or promoted had
to and have to be competitive not only in

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:35.090
Dubai, but they must be able to compete in
the international market.

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:38.940
This would generate sufficient profits to
enable this vehicle to continue investing

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:39.940
in new projects.

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:46.190
A good example is the case of Emirates.

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:51.361
It is clear that in 1979, when the Dubai World
Trade Center was inaugurated, Dubai had a

00:06:51.361 --> 00:06:54.010
clear problem: it was isolated from the world.

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:58.160
Who would want to make a long journey to reach
such an inhospitable place?

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:03.110
However, if you want to become a financial,
tourist and commercial center, you need one

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:04.289
thing: communications.

00:07:04.289 --> 00:07:09.949
To solve this problem, in March 1985 the Emirate
of Dubai created Emirates, an airline that

00:07:09.949 --> 00:07:14.870
would revolutionize intercontinental air transport
and make this city-emirate one of the best-connected

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:16.300
enclaves on the planet.

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:19.580
It doesn't matter if you want to fly to Tokyo,
Sydney or London.

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:21.960
In Dubai, you always have connections.

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:26.460
To achieve this, in early 1985, the Dubai
government gave British entrepreneur Maurice

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:31.500
Flanagan $10 million and as many administrative
facilities as possible.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:32.500
His goal?

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:36.580
To launch an airline in five months that had
to perform well and make money.

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:38.750
There would be no subsidies or extra protection.

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:42.590
The company had to fly, excuse the pun, on
its own.

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:47.620
And boy, did it deliver.

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:51.650
Today Emirates is one of the largest and most
profitable airlines in the world.

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:57.129
It operates in 148 airports around the world
and in 2022, despite the industry still reeling

00:07:57.129 --> 00:08:00.840
from the effects of the pandemic, it earned
almost $3 billion.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:04.870
But above all, Emirates has made Dubai one
of the best-connected places in the world.

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:09.520
This has made it easier for workers, entrepreneurs,
investors and, of course, tourists to come

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:10.639
to this city.

00:08:10.639 --> 00:08:11.710
Take a look.

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:20.069
(Aug 7, 2023: Dubai welcomes over 8.55mn visitors
in 6 months surpassing pre-pandemic levels

00:08:20.069 --> 00:08:21.069
- Arabian Business)

00:08:21.069 --> 00:08:25.060
And so, along with special economic zones,
low taxes and legal certainty, Dubai's success

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:28.830
has another key ingredient: the entrepreneurial
spirit of its government.

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:34.440
The key word here is entrepreneurial, not
to create inefficient and elephantine state-owned

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:38.370
companies, but to promote the creation of
companies that, while promoting the local

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:42.099
economy, need to make money and be very competitive,
whatever the cost.

00:08:42.099 --> 00:08:43.589
But, that's not even all.

00:08:43.589 --> 00:08:46.209
The idea of the entrepreneurial State goes
much further.

00:08:46.209 --> 00:08:50.170
In Dubai, the vast majority of projects go
ahead if they are likely to be profitable.

00:08:50.170 --> 00:08:52.550
And the government also supports private companies.

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:57.640
For example, one of its latest initiatives
has been to launch a fund of almost $300 million

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:01.470
to encourage 1,000 technology start-ups to
set up in Dubai.

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:02.470
So...

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:05.560
As you can see, we could almost say that this
emirate-city functions more like a company

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:07.230
than a conventional government.

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:10.100
And certainly today its success is undeniable.

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:12.600
Of course, this is not a unique case.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:16.120
Singapore's success also has a lot in common
with Dubai’s.

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:21.310
Let’s check it out.

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:30.680
(THE CITY OF THE MERLION)

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:35.040
In 1942 the Japanese took control of the British
enclave of Singapore.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.600
It is a strategically located city, which
the British Empire had turned into an important

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:42.459
naval base and which suffered greatly during
the Second World War.

00:09:42.459 --> 00:09:47.930
By the end of that war, the city was a disaster:
the damage suffered was enormous, many if

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:52.830
not most infrastructure was destroyed, and
mortality, violence and corruption rates were

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:54.019
sky high.

00:09:54.019 --> 00:09:58.010
Singapore was a very poor city where virtually
no one in their right mind would want to live.

00:09:58.010 --> 00:09:59.199
And do you know what?

00:09:59.199 --> 00:10:01.029
The future didn't look much better.

00:10:01.029 --> 00:10:05.930
As it turned out, the Korean War and the global
demand for tin and rubber made things better

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:08.740
and Singapore regained its role as a trading
port.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:09.790
But little else.

00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:12.760
There was even the threat of a communist revolution
in the city.

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:19.160
In 1958 the city achieved full autonomy, and
in 1963, after declaring its definitive independence

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:22.459
from the British Empire, it was integrated
into Malaysia.

00:10:22.459 --> 00:10:24.630
However, things did not work out.

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:28.089
In 1965 the Malaysian federation threw Singapore
out.

00:10:28.089 --> 00:10:30.910
And so the country would begin its journey
alone...

00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:33.160
Which was not particularly optimistic.

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:36.930
Singapore was basically an impoverished island
with no natural resources.

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:40.779
It did not even have enough drinking water
to supply its population.

00:10:40.779 --> 00:10:42.279
Water had to be imported from Malaysia.

00:10:42.279 --> 00:10:48.850
These days, of course, things are totally
different.

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:53.611
Over the following 60 years, Singapore experienced
an unprecedented boom that has led it to become

00:10:53.611 --> 00:10:55.730
the fourth richest country in the world.

00:10:55.730 --> 00:11:00.200
A tremendously prosperous place with one of
the highest standards of living on the planet.

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:03.900
Of course, as we have already told you on
VisualPolitik, openness to foreign investment,

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:08.829
a high level of legal certainty, relatively
low taxes and a clear vocation for savings

00:11:08.829 --> 00:11:10.300
were key elements.

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:13.930
However, Singapore's success has also had
another ingredient,

00:11:13.930 --> 00:11:18.570
As we have seen in the case of Dubai, the
government of Singapore also had a clear entrepreneurial

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:19.570
calling...

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:25.389
In 1974 the Singapore government founded TEMASEK,
a kind of sovereign wealth fund or investment

00:11:25.389 --> 00:11:29.770
arm that today manages assets worth almost
$300 billion US dollars.

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:32.990
That is more than US$50,000 per citizen.

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:36.460
The most curious thing, however, is that the
Singapore government created this financial

00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:41.019
vehicle to bring together state-owned companies
and to keep politicians and civil servants

00:11:41.019 --> 00:11:42.019
away from them.

00:11:42.019 --> 00:11:46.440
The idea at that time was that the 35 companies
owned by or receiving participation from the

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:51.300
State would function as true private companies,
even though they still belonged to the State.

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:55.649
That was the beginning, but then with reinvested
profits they started investing in new businesses

00:11:55.649 --> 00:11:57.019
to fuel their growth.

00:11:57.019 --> 00:12:02.360
Up until 2002, TEMASEK concentrated the vast
majority of its investments in Singapore,

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:06.430
but then they started investing in the rest
of the world, particularly in Asia.

00:12:06.430 --> 00:12:10.579
The idea is that these investments in foreign
companies created or strengthened corporate

00:12:10.579 --> 00:12:12.230
ties with Singapore itself.

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:16.220
For example, almost a quarter of all their
investments are in China.

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:19.790
As it turned out, over the years, TEMASEK
not only made it possible to professionalize

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:24.019
state-owned companies, but it has also become
a huge piggy bank for the government and has

00:12:24.019 --> 00:12:26.579
helped to propel a lot of new companies.

00:12:26.579 --> 00:12:29.300
Especially in sectors considered strategic
for the city.

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:31.100
Do you remember what we said about Emirates
before?

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:33.110
Well, something similar happened in Singapore.

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:38.530
TEMASEK is the largest shareholder of Singapore
Airlines, holding 55% of the shares.

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:42.161
With its help, this airline not only connected
Singapore with the rest of the world, but

00:12:42.161 --> 00:12:44.540
also became one of the most reputable airlines.

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:49.740
And what is even better, is that it also became
one of the most valuable and profitable airlines

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:50.740
on the planet.

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:54.630
The estimated profit for 2023 is close to
$2 billion US dollars.

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:57.190
And the same could be said of many other companies.

00:12:57.190 --> 00:13:01.240
VisualPolitik viewers, with these kinds of
strategies, both Dubai and Singapore achieved

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:02.510
several objectives:

00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:05.850
Firstly, they filled some important gaps,
such as communications.

00:13:05.850 --> 00:13:10.890
Secondly, they contributed to the professionalization
of state-owned companies.

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:15.889
("One of the tragic illusions that many countries
of the Third World entertain is the notion

00:13:15.889 --> 00:13:20.450
that politicians and civil servants can successfully
perform entrepreneurial functions.

00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:25.570
It is curious that, in the face of overwhelming
evidence to the contrary, the belief persists."

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:26.570
– Dr Goh Keng Swee, the first Finance Minister
of Singapore)

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:30.560
Thirdly, they helped boost and develop a lot
of economic sectors, which in turn attracted

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:33.209
a lot of foreign companies and professionals.

00:13:33.209 --> 00:13:38.380
In other words, by themselves, these investment
arms have driven – and still drive – a

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:44.030
lot of foreign investment.

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:48.100
And lastly, they have created a huge piggy
bank that generates billions of dollars in

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:49.300
profits each year.

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:53.220
And don't forget that we are talking about
two relatively small territories.

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:58.860
Dubai has a population, including all foreign
professionals, of only about 3.5 million people.

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:01.620
Singapore, in contrast, about 5.5 million.

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:03.899
And yet, the numbers we are looking at are
formidable.

00:14:03.899 --> 00:14:08.000
Not surprisingly, in addition to creating
these investment arms, both governments opened

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:12.759
their doors wide to foreign companies and
promoted entrepreneurship like there was no

00:14:12.759 --> 00:14:13.759
tomorrow.

00:14:13.759 --> 00:14:17.810
So, the question we can ask ourselves is,
could this same model be replicated to help

00:14:17.810 --> 00:14:19.389
develop much poorer regions?

00:14:19.389 --> 00:14:24.180
Would it be possible to create similar cities
in places in Africa or even Latin America?

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:26.649
And remember this is no trivial matter.

00:14:26.649 --> 00:14:31.570
For instance, the lack of legal security,
openness and connectivity currently make Miami

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:36.930
the headquarters for the Latin American activities
of more than 1,400 multinationals.

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:41.120
If Latin America were as business-friendly
as Dubai or Singapore, it is quite possible

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:45.770
that the vast majority of these multinationals
would not be in Miami, but in that other city.

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:50.080
On the other hand, while Dubai and Singapore
have built huge piggy banks that have also

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:54.550
promoted economic development, Latin American
commodity-exporting countries have not been

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:55.550
so lucky.

00:14:55.550 --> 00:14:58.190
Of course, Venezuela is a textbook example.

00:14:58.190 --> 00:15:02.190
We are talking about tens of billions of dollars
that were squandered instead of contributing

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:03.389
to national development.

00:15:03.389 --> 00:15:07.800
Be that as it may, at this point it is over
to you: What do you think of the entrepreneurial

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:09.780
governance model of Dubai and Singapore?

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:14.300
Do you think that formulas like these could
be replicated in Latin America or Africa?

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:15.630
Leave us your answer in the comments.

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:19.440
I hope that you found this new partnership
between VisualPolitik and Harvard International

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:21.430
Review as interesting as we did.

00:15:21.430 --> 00:15:25.100
If so, don't forget to like and subscribe
to the channel if you haven't already done

00:15:25.100 --> 00:15:26.100
so.

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:27.110
Thank you very much for watching.

00:15:27.110 --> 00:15:28.399
All the best and see you next time.

